XMMS 1.3.0
==========

XMMS 1.2.11 was released in 2007, on GTK+ 1.2. This is XMMS
built against CGTK, the Classic GUI Toolkit, which is GTK+ 1.2
brought to current systems, and brought up to date itself. It
keeps its look, its skins and its keys; what follows is what
has changed since 1.2.11.

Toolkit and windows
* Built against CGTK 1.4. Runs on X11 and on Wayland, whichever
  CGTK was built for; GDK_BACKEND chooses when both are available.
* On Wayland the player, equalizer and playlist are one window
  of the compositor's, in a layout chosen in Preferences ->
  Options or from the Layout menu: the playlist beside the
  player, or below the equalizer. The playlist's size is
  remembered for each layout, and hiding or shading a window
  closes the gap it leaves.
* Minimising, "always on top" and "show on all desktops" go
  through GDK rather than X directly; the X-specific code is
  gone, and with it wmxmms, the Window Maker dock applet.
* Saved window positions are kept on the screen.
* The sources, the desktop file and the translations are UTF-8.
  Playlist titles are drawn as wide characters, so every script
  shows. Fonts are named as fontconfig names them ("Sans 10");
  the "Use fontsets" option is gone.
* The playlist's popup menus are in the playlist window and
  stay up after a click. The equalizer's sliders move from the
  keyboard (` and Tab, 1-0 and q-p), and Control, Alt and Shift
  combinations keep their global meaning in the equalizer.
* xmms.desktop is installed, naming the audio types XMMS plays,
  so desktops offer XMMS for them.

Audio
* The output pipeline is new. Input plugins hand decoded audio
  to XMMS, which runs the equalizer and the effect plugins over
  it; one thread owns the output plugin and feeds it. If the
  device is busy when a song starts, XMMS waits rather than
  failing. Each output plugin's buffering is set in its own
  dialogue.
* Volume and balance are XMMS's own, applied as the audio leaves
  for the device, and never touch a hardware mixer, so they work
  the same through every output plugin and through sound
  servers. The percentages follow the curve PulseAudio and
  PipeWire use: 50% is about -18 dB.
* The ten band equalizer is part of XMMS and works on every
  format; before, only the MPEG audio plugin had one. It is a
  cascade of biquad filters whose gains are solved so that each
  slider is the gain at its band's centre.
* New input plugins: Opus (libopusfile), FLAC (libFLAC, with
  ReplayGain and dithering), and MPEG-4 Audio: AAC in MP4 and
  ADTS files and streams, decoded by faad2, and Apple Lossless,
  decoded by XMMS's own code, gapless when the file says how.
* MPEG audio is decoded by the system's libmpg123, not the
  decoder that was bundled. The x86 assembly is gone.
* The Wave plugin plays PCM of 8 to 32 bits, 32 and 64 bit
  floating point, and A-law and mu-law.
* Streaming is one implementation shared by every input plugin:
  http and https (OpenSSL or LibreSSL), proxies with
  authentication, saving the stream to disk, and SHOUTcast and
  Icecast titles. A secure stream's redirect to an insecure one
  is refused. Hosts are resolved with getaddrinfo, so IPv6 works
  without a configure option.
* A sndio output plugin, for OpenBSD and systems that carry
  sndio. The OSS plugin uses the system's <sys/soundcard.h>.
  The ESD, Sun and Solaris output plugins are gone.
* The OpenGL spectrum analyzer draws in a GtkGLArea, in step with
  the display, on X11 and Wayland alike; the 3Dfx fullscreen mode
  is gone.
* The CD audio plugin and the IRman plugin are gone.
* The realtime priority option and the setuid code behind it are
  gone. XMMS runs as the user who starts it.

Files
* XMMS keeps its files in the XDG base directories:
  configuration in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xmms (~/.config/xmms) and
  skins and user plugins in $XDG_DATA_HOME/xmms
  (~/.local/share/xmms/Skins, ~/.local/share/xmms/Plugins).
  ~/.xmms is not read. The control socket is still
  /tmp/xmms_USER.N.
* Skin archives (.wsz, .zip, .tar, .tar.gz, .tgz) are read by
  XMMS itself, in memory, with no unzip or tar: only the skin's
  images and text files are kept, and nothing in an archive
  reaches the file system. BMP headers the decoders cannot take
  are refused.
* New skins: Bloodrose, Innercity, Candyflip, and Grand.
* The plugins are named as Winamp named its: in_*, out_*, dsp_*,
  gen_* and vis_*. Their libtool archives are not installed.

Building and code
* One configure, regenerated by whoever builds from git with
  autoreconf; CGTK 1.4 is found with pkg-config. ISO C99 and
  POSIX; the checks for what they guarantee are gone, as are the
  bundled getopt, mkdtemp and libintl.
* Builds without warnings at -Wall with gcc and clang,
  and many of the warnings were bugs: sign mismatches that let
  negative values through, unbounded string functions, memory
  leaks, a main thread that could block on a song file, races
  in the playlist and at shutdown.
* Keywords are compared as ASCII and names sorted by the locale,
  whatever the locale is.
* The joystick plugin defaults to /dev/input/js0 and js1.

Translations
* The 55 catalogues are UTF-8, under current language codes,
  merged with the current sources, and complete. The strings
  added since 1.2.11, and those the old catalogues had left
  untranslated or fuzzy, were translated by machine and have not
  been reviewed by native speakers; corrections are welcome.
* Fixed in the old translations: menu items whose path did not
  match their submenu's translation, which gave them a second
  submenu of their own; format strings that did not match the
  original, a crash risk; text damaged by old character set
  conversions; and Irish "Yes" and "No", which were swapped.

Project
* 1.2.11's README, NEWS, ChangeLog, AUTHORS, FAQ, TODO and
  other notes are in history/xmms, as they were released.
* The xmms.org addresses are gone, as are xmms.spec and
  xmms.wmconfig, and the README is revised.
